{"ts": 1789779332.5550919, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "Sign checks confirm [+1,+1,+1] is correct for {1,7,9} \u2014 flips degrade sharply (0.44, 0.37, 0.16), which is a good sign the signal is real rather than sign-fitted noise.\n\n**Selection: features {1, 7, 9}, all long, IS Sharpe 0.971.**\n\nA note on my reasoning for the OOS forecast. I searched roughly 80 specifications, so some of the IS number is selection bias. The useful decomposition:\n\n- Features 1, 7, 9 each stand alone at 0.71 / 0.52 / 0.47 \u2014 these were the top 3 of 40 marginals by a wide margin, and they survived without needing a lucky sign. Their standalone strength is the part I trust most.\n- The pairwise combos (0.87, 0.83, 0.68) and the triple (0.97) show genuine diversification: the triple beats the best single by ~0.26. That gain is consistent with three partially-independent signals, not with overfitting a single lucky interaction.\n- The competing third-slot candidates clustered tightly at 0.83\u20130.95. When many alternatives land near the winner, the winner's *margin* is mostly noise even though its *level* is mostly real. So I shade 0.971 down, but not dramatically.\n\nSelection bias from ~80 draws on n=5000 is modest: the per-spec standard error is roughly 1/\u221a(5000/252) \u2248 0.22, and picking the max of many correlated draws inflates by perhaps 0.15\u20130.25. I center my belief near 0.72 and use a fairly wide SD of 0.27 to cover the chance that some of the marginal edge is regime-specific and does not persist."}
