{"ts": 1789779375.293731, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted specification:** long features **1, 7, 9** (equal weight, all positive sign).\n\n**Method:**\n1. **Marginal screen** \u2014 evaluated all 40 features individually. Features 1 (0.711), 7 (0.515), and 9 (0.465) were clear outliers; the rest of the distribution sat within roughly \u00b10.27, consistent with noise around zero.\n2. **Pairwise** \u2014 tested the top-3 against each other and feature 1 against ~17 other candidates. Best pairs were {1,7}=0.871 and {1,9}=0.833.\n3. **Triples** \u2014 swept the third slot across ~20 candidates for both {1,7,\u00b7} and {1,9,\u00b7}. The natural combination {1,7,9}=0.971 beat every alternative.\n4. **Sign validation** \u2014 flipping any one sign collapsed performance (0.443, 0.374, 0.164), confirming the directions are driven by signal rather than fitted to noise.\n\n**Forecast: mean 0.72, SD 0.27.**\n\nThe discount from 0.971 reflects selection bias across ~80 evaluations. I weighted two pieces of evidence in opposite directions: the three features' *standalone* strength and the sharp asymmetry under sign flips argue the core signal is real and should largely persist; but the third-slot candidates clustered tightly at 0.83\u20130.95, meaning the specific *margin* by which {1,7,9} won is mostly luck of the draw. Hence the level is credible while the peak is not \u2014 I shade toward the middle of that cluster rather than taking the maximum at face value.\n\nThe SD of 0.27 is deliberately wide. Beyond ordinary sampling error (~0.22 per spec at n=5000), it covers a risk the in-sample search cannot detect at all: that these three features derive their edge from a market regime present in the sample and absent out-of-sample. No amount of additional in-sample evaluation would resolve that, which is why I stopped searching rather than continuing to mine for a higher IS number."}
